'kashmir-by-season' => array(
    'title' => 'Kashmir by season: what each month is like',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'kashmir',
    'desc' => 'Tulips in spring, green meadows in summer, gold in autumn and snow in winter: how to choose your month for Kashmir and what changes in the itinerary.',
    'lead' => 'Kashmir changes completely through the year. The same Srinagar–Gulmarg–Pahalgam route feels like four different trips depending on when you go.',
    'sections' => array(
        array('Spring (March to May): tulips and fresh green', '<p>Spring is when Srinagar is at its most colourful. The Indira Gandhi Memorial Tulip Garden usually blooms from late March into April, and the Mughal gardens (Nishat and Shalimar Bagh) come back to life. Days are pleasant; mornings and evenings are still cool.</p><p>In Gulmarg and Sonmarg, snow can linger into April, which gives you both snow and flowers in the same trip. Carry light woollens and a jacket.</p>'),
        array('Summer (June to August): meadows and long days', '<p>Summer is peak season. Pahalgam\'s valleys, Gulmarg\'s meadows and Sonmarg are green and open, and long daylight hours make day trips from Srinagar comfortable. It is also the busiest time, so hotels and houseboats fill early; book well ahead.</p><p>The Amarnath Yatra usually runs in July and August, which affects traffic around Pahalgam, Sonmarg and Baltal. Plan your sightseeing days around it.</p>'),
        array('Autumn (September to November): chinar gold', '<p>Autumn is many travellers\' favourite. The chinar trees turn red and gold, skies are clear, and the crowds thin out after summer. It is ideal for photography and for a relaxed pace. Nights get cold by November, so pack warm layers.</p>'),
        array('Winter (December to February): snow', '<p>Winter is for snow lovers. Gulmarg becomes a ski destination and the Gondola rides over white slopes. Roads to Sonmarg and higher areas can close after heavy snowfall, so winter itineraries keep day trips flexible. Heavy woollens, thermals and waterproof boots are essential.</p>'),
        array('How many days you need', '<p>Four nights covers Srinagar, Gulmarg and Pahalgam comfortably, as in our <strong>Srinagar Gulmarg Pahalgam</strong> 4 nights / 5 days package. Five or six nights add Sonmarg without rushing, and a 7-night plan can continue to Katra for Mata Vaishno Devi.</p>'),
        array('How to reach Kashmir', '<p>Srinagar airport (SXR) has direct flights from Delhi and several other Indian cities, and almost every Kashmir itinerary starts and ends there. By rail, the network reaches Jammu and beyond; from Jammu, Srinagar is a long road journey through the mountains, so most visitors fly. If you plan to add Mata Vaishno Devi, a common route is to fly into Srinagar and leave from Jammu after Katra, as in our 7-night plan.</p>'),
        array('Month by month at a glance', '<ul><li><strong>January–February:</strong> deep winter; snow in Gulmarg and often in Srinagar; some higher roads closed.</li><li><strong>March:</strong> the thaw begins; cold nights; the first blossoms in Srinagar.</li><li><strong>April:</strong> tulip season and spring gardens; snow still on the higher slopes.</li><li><strong>May:</strong> mild and fresh; meadows turning green; the start of the busy season.</li><li><strong>June–July:</strong> peak summer season; long days; the Amarnath Yatra begins around July.</li><li><strong>August:</strong> green valleys; occasional rain; yatra traffic on some routes.</li><li><strong>September–October:</strong> clear skies and autumn colour; one of the best times for photography.</li><li><strong>November:</strong> late autumn and cold nights; quieter hotels.</li><li><strong>December:</strong> winter arrives; the first snow in Gulmarg.</li></ul>'),
        array('What to pack by season', '<ul><li><strong>Spring (March–May):</strong> light woollens, a jacket, a rain layer and comfortable walking shoes.</li><li><strong>Summer (June–August):</strong> cotton clothes for the day, plus a fleece or jacket for evenings and for Gulmarg and Sonmarg; sunscreen and sunglasses.</li><li><strong>Autumn (September–November):</strong> layers: a warm sweater and a jacket for cool mornings and evenings.</li><li><strong>Winter (December–February):</strong> heavy winter wear, thermal layers, a down jacket, gloves, a cap and waterproof boots for snow.</li></ul>'),
        array('Houseboat or hotel?', '<p>A night or two on a Dal Lake or Nigeen Lake houseboat is part of the Kashmir experience: carved wooden interiors, a shikara to the shore, and quiet mornings on the water. Many travellers combine one houseboat night with hotels for the rest of the trip. In winter, ask about heating before booking a houseboat; hotels are usually warmer.</p>'),
        array('Practical tips', '<ul><li><strong>Mobile phones:</strong> prepaid SIMs from outside Jammu and Kashmir usually do not work there; postpaid connections generally do.</li><li><strong>Local rides:</strong> the Gulmarg Gondola, ponies and union taxis in Pahalgam are paid locally and are not part of package prices.</li><li><strong>Shopping:</strong> pashmina, papier-mâché, walnut-wood carvings, carpets, dry fruits and saffron; buy from government emporiums or trusted shops.</li><li><strong>Plan with buffers:</strong> weather can close high roads for a day, so keep your flight home in the afternoon or add a spare day.</li></ul>'),
    ),
    'faqs' => array(
        array('What is the best month to visit Kashmir?', '<p>April for tulips and spring colour, June for green meadows, October for autumn colour, and January for snow. There is no single best month; it depends on what you want to see.</p>'),
        array('Is Kashmir too cold in winter for families?', '<p>Not if you pack properly and keep the plan flexible. Hotels are heated, and a slower itinerary with Gulmarg snow days works well for families.</p>'),        array('How many days do I need for Kashmir?', '<p>Four nights for Srinagar, Gulmarg and Pahalgam; five to six nights to add Sonmarg or go slower; and seven nights to add Mata Vaishno Devi.</p>'),
        array('Can I see snow in Kashmir in summer?', '<p>Usually yes, at higher points. In late spring and early summer there is often snow near the top of the Gulmarg Gondola and around Sonmarg\'s Thajiwas glacier, depending on the year.</p>'),

    ),
),

'srinagar-gulmarg-pahalgam-itinerary' => array(
    'title' => 'Srinagar, Gulmarg and Pahalgam: how to plan 5 days',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'kashmir',
    'desc' => 'A practical 5-day Kashmir plan: Srinagar sightseeing, Gulmarg, Pahalgam and where to stay each night, with the drive times from our own itineraries.',
    'lead' => 'Five days is the classic first trip to Kashmir. Here is how we usually arrange it, and why the order matters.',
    'sections' => array(
        array('Day 1: Arrive in Srinagar', '<p>Fly into Srinagar and settle in. If you are staying on a houseboat, the first evening is perfect for a shikara ride on Dal Lake as the lights come on.</p>'),
        array('Day 2: Srinagar sightseeing', '<p>A full day for the Mughal gardens (Nishat Bagh, Shalimar Bagh and Chashme Shahi), the old city and Dal Lake. Keep time for a slow shikara ride and the floating market area if you are up early.</p>'),
        array('Day 3: Srinagar to Gulmarg (about 56 km)', '<p>Gulmarg is roughly a 2-hour drive. The highlight is the Gulmarg Gondola, which runs in two phases; tickets are bought locally and sell out on busy days, so start early. In winter, this is your snow day.</p>'),
        array('Day 4: Gulmarg to Pahalgam', '<p>This is the longest drive of the trip, about 149 km. Pahalgam sits along the Lidder river, and the local valleys (Aru, Betaab and Chandanwari) are reached by local taxi, which is paid locally.</p>'),
        array('Day 5: Pahalgam to Srinagar and departure', '<p>Drive back to Srinagar for your flight. Allow a buffer: morning flights after a Pahalgam night are tight, so we prefer afternoon departures.</p>'),
        array('Variations', '<ul><li><strong>Day trips only:</strong> stay all nights in Srinagar and visit Gulmarg and Sonmarg as day trips (our Srinagar Gulmarg Sonmarg Day Trip tour).</li><li><strong>Add Sonmarg:</strong> one extra day for the glacier valley.</li><li><strong>Add Vaishno Devi:</strong> continue from Srinagar to Katra (about 268 km) for the yatra.</li></ul>'),
    ),
    'faqs' => array(
        array('Is 4 nights enough for Kashmir?', '<p>Yes for Srinagar, Gulmarg and Pahalgam. Add one night for Sonmarg, or two to go at a slower pace.</p>'),
    ),
),

'gulmarg-pahalgam-sonmarg-compared' => array(
    'title' => 'Gulmarg, Pahalgam or Sonmarg: which to choose',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'kashmir',
    'desc' => 'Gulmarg, Pahalgam and Sonmarg compared: what each is best for, how far they are from Srinagar and whether to stay overnight or visit on a day trip.',
    'lead' => 'All three are beautiful, but they suit different travellers. If you only have time for one or two, this helps you choose.',
    'sections' => array(
        array('Gulmarg: the Gondola and snow', '<p>About 56 km from Srinagar. Gulmarg is known for its meadow and the Gulmarg Gondola, one of the highest cable cars in the world. In winter it is Kashmir\'s snow and ski centre. It works well as a day trip, or as an overnight stay if you want the Gondola early before crowds.</p>'),
        array('Pahalgam: river valleys and a slower pace', '<p>Pahalgam sits by the Lidder river, with side valleys such as Aru, Betaab and Chandanwari. It suits families and travellers who like walks and greenery. Because it is further (roughly a 186 km round trip from Srinagar), an overnight stay is more relaxed than a day trip.</p>'),
        array('Sonmarg: the glacier valley', '<p>Sonmarg, the "meadow of gold", is on the road towards Zoji La. From here, ponies go up to the Thajiwas glacier. It is usually done as a day trip from Srinagar (roughly a 196 km round trip). Heavy snow can close the road in winter.</p>'),
        array('Our suggestion', '<ul><li><strong>First visit, 4 nights:</strong> Gulmarg and Pahalgam.</li><li><strong>Snow in winter:</strong> Gulmarg first.</li><li><strong>Summer with children:</strong> Pahalgam overnight, Gulmarg as a day trip.</li><li><strong>5 nights or more:</strong> all three.</li></ul>'),
        array('Local transport rules', '<p>In Pahalgam and at some points in Gulmarg and Sonmarg, sightseeing beyond the main town is by local union taxis or ponies, paid locally. Your package cab takes you to the town; the local rides are extra.</p>'),
    ),
    'faqs' => array(),
),

'leh-ladakh-first-trip-acclimatisation' => array(
    'title' => 'Leh Ladakh for first-timers: acclimatisation and planning',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'ladakh',
    'desc' => 'Planning a first Leh Ladakh trip: why the first day is for rest, how to acclimatise safely, when to go and how many days you need.',
    'lead' => 'Ladakh is high-altitude country. Planning your first two days properly is the single most important thing for a good trip.',
    'sections' => array(
        array('Why the first day is for rest', '<p>Leh is at roughly 3,500 metres. If you fly in from the plains, your body needs time to adjust to the thinner air. That is why every one of our Ladakh itineraries starts with "Leh arrival" and nothing else planned: rest, drink water, and take a short, easy walk in the evening at most.</p>'),
        array('Signs of altitude sickness', '<p>Headache, nausea, dizziness, breathlessness at rest and poor sleep are common signs. Mild symptoms usually settle with rest. If they get worse, tell your driver or hotel immediately and go lower; do not continue to higher passes. Talk to your doctor before the trip, especially if you have heart, lung or blood-pressure conditions.</p>'),
        array('Go higher gradually', '<p>We keep the first sightseeing day lower and nearby (monasteries or Sham Valley) before crossing high passes such as Khardung La on the way to Nubra, or Chang La towards Pangong. Sleeping at a lower altitude than the highest point you visit also helps.</p>'),
        array('When to go', '<p>The main season is roughly May to September, when the roads over the high passes are generally open. Early and late season can be very cold at night. Winter trips are possible, but roads and facilities are limited.</p>'),
        array('How many days', '<ul><li><strong>4 nights:</strong> Leh, monasteries, Khardung La and Pangong or Sham Valley.</li><li><strong>5–6 nights:</strong> adds Nubra Valley overnight.</li><li><strong>7–8 nights:</strong> Nubra and Pangong overnight, plus Tso Moriri in our longest plan.</li></ul>'),
        array('Permits', '<p>Areas such as Nubra, Pangong and Tso Moriri need permits, and the rules differ for Indian and foreign nationals. We arrange them as part of the itinerary and check the current rules for your dates.</p>'),
    ),
    'faqs' => array(
        array('Can children and senior citizens visit Ladakh?', '<p>Many do, with a slower plan and a doctor\'s advice beforehand. We add rest time and avoid long days at altitude for them.</p>'),
    ),
),

'nubra-pangong-ladakh-route' => array(
    'title' => 'Nubra Valley and Pangong Lake: how to route your Ladakh trip',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'ladakh',
    'desc' => 'How to plan Nubra Valley and Pangong Lake from Leh: distances and drive times, whether to stay overnight, and the order we recommend.',
    'lead' => 'Nubra and Pangong are the two big excursions from Leh. The order and overnight stays make a big difference to comfort.',
    'sections' => array(
        array('Leh to Nubra Valley', '<p>Nubra is reached over Khardung La, one of the highest motorable passes. The drive back from Nubra to Leh is roughly 120 km and 4–5 hours in our itineraries. In Nubra you see Diskit monastery, the sand dunes at Hunder and the double-humped Bactrian camels.</p>'),
        array('Leh to Pangong Lake', '<p>Pangong is roughly 140 km from Leh, about 4–5 hours each way. The lake changes colour through the day, from deep blue to turquoise. You can visit it as a long day trip, but staying overnight lets you see it in the soft light of evening and early morning.</p>'),
        array('Overnight or day trip?', '<p>Day trips save a night but mean 8–10 hours on the road in one day at altitude. Overnight stays in Nubra and at Pangong are easier on the body and much more rewarding. Camps and simple stays at Pangong are cold at night, even in summer.</p>'),
        array('Our recommended order', '<ol><li>Arrive Leh and rest.</li><li>Leh monasteries or Sham Valley (lower, for acclimatisation).</li><li>Leh to Nubra over Khardung La (overnight).</li><li>Nubra back to Leh.</li><li>Leh to Pangong (overnight).</li><li>Pangong back to Leh.</li><li>Departure.</li></ol><p>This is the shape of our Panorama Ladakh tour (6 nights / 7 days).</p>'),
    ),
    'faqs' => array(
        array('Is there mobile network at Pangong and Nubra?', '<p>Coverage is limited and usually only on some postpaid networks. Plan to be offline for part of those days.</p>'),
    ),
),

'shimla-manali-first-trip' => array(
